{"status":"confirmed","reasoning":"For f(x)=e^{3x}, the nth derivative at zero is 3^n. Thus P_4(x)=sum from n=0 to 4 of (3^n/n!)x^n=1+3x+(9/2)x^2+(9/2)x^3+(27/8)x^4. This is option b and agrees with Math Academy's worked solution.","confidence":1,"answers":[{"key":"selection","correct_value":"1+3x+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{4}","correct_option":"b","value_type":"math","wrong_value":"1+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{81}{4}{x}^{4}","correct_keys":[],"wrong_keys":[],"ma_value":"1+3x+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{4}","ma_basis":"worked_solution","ma_evidence":"\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =1+3\\cdot x+\\frac{9}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{27}{3!}{x}^{3}+\\frac{81}{4!}{x}^{4} \\\\  & =1+3x+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}","disagrees":false}]}